  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">INCREASING PROTECTION OF FOREST ELEPHANT POPULATIONS IN KEY PROTECTED AREAS AND INTERZONES IN THE NORTHERN TRIDOM LANDSCAPE, CAMEROON</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of this project is to document elephant abundance and movement in southeast Cameroon and to improve surveillance and protection of a heavily poached transfrontier population that extends into Gabon and the Republic of Congo. Forest elephants have declined by over 60% since 2002 and by over 80% at sites like Gabons Minkebe National Park, a former stronghold that lost an estimated 25,000 elephants to poaching from 2004 to 2014. Cross-border poaching networks primarily originating out of Cameroon are driving the decline, and few sites have enough data to determine the status of remaining elephants and priorities for future conservation action. This effort helps fill that critical knowledge gap by surveying five protected areas on or near the Cameroon-Gabon border, assessing population connectivity, and supporting a protection presence at select sites. Specific activities include: (1) estimation of elephant populations in Boumba-Bek/Nki/Kom National Parks and nearby areas; (2) deployment of camera traps and other technologies to document illegal activities and elephant movement between protected areas; (3) collection of elephant dung for genetic analysis of population relatedness and connectivity; and (4) support for ground patrols and a protected area needs assessment in consultation with park managers.</narrative>
  </description>
